data.content:
Use EF Core 7+'s **`ExecuteUpdateAsync`** and **`ExecuteDeleteAsync`** — they run a single SQL statement server-side without loading entities into memory:

```csharp
// Operation 1: Bulk update — single UPDATE statement
await _db.Products
    .Where(p => p.LastSoldDate < DateTime.UtcNow.AddMonths(-6))
    .ExecuteUpdateAsync(s => s.SetProperty(p => p.IsActive, false));

// Operation 2: Bulk delete — single DELETE statement
await _db.AuditLogs
    .Where(l => l.CreatedAt < DateTime.UtcNow.AddYears(-1))
    .ExecuteDeleteAsync();
```

**Why this is fast:**
- Generates a single `UPDATE`/`DELETE` SQL statement
- No entities loaded into memory (zero tracking overhead)
- No `SaveChangesAsync()` needed — executes immediately
- Goes from O(n) memory + n round-trips → O(1) memory + 1 round-trip

**Generated SQL (approximately):**
```sql
UPDATE Products SET IsActive = 0 WHERE LastSoldDate < @cutoff;
DELETE FROM AuditLogs WHERE CreatedAt < @cutoff;
```

**Caveats:**
- Bypasses change tracker — any cached entities won't reflect the change
- Bypasses `SaveChanges` interceptors/events
- If you have EF interceptors or domain events on these operations, you'll need a different approach

For the 2M audit log delete, if it causes lock escalation, batch it:

```csharp
int deleted;
do
{
    deleted = await _db.AuditLogs
        .Where(l => l.CreatedAt < DateTime.UtcNow.AddYears(-1))
        .Take(10_000)
        .ExecuteDeleteAsync();
} while (deleted > 0);
```
